From: ReArc Authors
Subject: [PATCH] P-TAB-001 built-in content blocking: "ReArc Blocker" component extension (ABI 1.66)

Engine side of docs/FEATURE-SPECS/CONTENT-BLOCKING.md, phase 1. Blocking is
Chromium's declarativeNetRequest: the lists of a blocklist bundle are the
static rulesets of a component extension, "ReArc Blocker" (fixed manifest
keys, IDs eoaoomnoofleafmoihlpjdhdceloddjm and
kanaeoiolklalbbkdlpbfohpmgkgackf, added to the component allowlist), which
ComponentLoader loads in every profile the host has switched blocking on
for. There are two IDs so that a list update can load the new bundle before
the old one goes (below); in ordinary times a profile has one blocker.
A component extension is not in the extensions page or
chrome.management and cannot be removed or disabled; it declares no action,
so it is not in the toolbar (ReArcTabGetExtensionActions also leaves both
out by ID); it runs in private windows ("incognito": "split", and component
extensions are always incognito-enabled).

The host owns every setting. ReArcEngineSetBlocklistDirectory names a bundle
it has verified, ReArcProfileSetContentBlocking the level and lists of a
profile, ReArcProfileSetBlockingExceptions its excepted sites and
ReArcProfileSetOwnBlockingRules the person's own rules. The engine keeps
what it was told in memory only, and a profile the host has said nothing
about has no blocker: an existing install's pages do not change until the
host switches blocking on.

What Chromium needed worked around, all in rearc_content_blocker.cc:

- A component extension's rulesets are not indexed at install
  (ExtensionRegistrar::AddComponentExtension passes no ruleset install
  prefs), so a ruleset enabled in the manifest fails to load for want of a
  checksum. The manifest the engine builds enables none; the lists are
  switched on with RulesMonitorService::UpdateEnabledStaticRulesets, which
  indexes a ruleset on first use and records its checksum. An empty update
  first marks the end of the extension's own load, then only the difference
  between the loaded and the wanted rulesets is applied.
- The index is written next to the rules (<extension>/_metadata/
  generated_indexed_rulesets), and the host's bundle may be inside the
  signed app. The engine copies the rules to
  <user data dir>/ReArc Blocker/<version> (blocking pool, once per version,
  a "complete" marker written last) and loads the extension from there.
- The extension version is the bundle version (202611180857 as
  2026.1118.857): a new bundle is an update, which clears the old checksums
  and enabled set in ExtensionPrefs.
- ComponentLoader::Remove is an uninstall to RulesMonitorService, which
  deletes the dynamic rules; they are set again from the host's state after
  every load.
- An extension cannot change the files of its rulesets without a reload, and
  a reload leaves every open page unblocked while the new bundle's rules are
  indexed. On a list update the new bundle is therefore loaded as the other
  blocker, beside the one that is blocking. The old one is removed when the
  new one has its site exceptions, its own rules and its lists on
  (FinishTakeover), in the order of the next point.
  A blocker that never got ready is replaced in place, a level change during
  an update removes the old blocker at once, and a new bundle that does not
  load or whose lists do not come on leaves the old one blocking. Both
  blockers' lists are on for that moment, which the bundle's size must allow
  (build-blocklists.py check_limits). Security CB-02.
- RulesMonitorService keeps two queues per extension, one for dynamic rule
  updates (the site exceptions and the own rules) and one for ruleset
  updates (the lists), and nothing orders one against the other. Started
  together, a blocker's lists could come on before its site exceptions, and
  a site the person excepted because blocking breaks it would have requests
  blocked for a moment. A blocker that is loaded therefore gets its site
  exceptions and own rules first, and its lists are switched on only when
  both dynamic rule updates have ended and their rules are in the matcher
  (SwitchListsOn), at profile load and in a takeover alike. An update
  Chromium refuses counts as ended: the lists still come on. What is left of
  the window: when the host changes the exceptions later, the lists are
  already on and the change takes the time of one dynamic rule update; and
  at a launch with the bundle of the last session Chromium itself loads the
  lists that were on together with the dynamic rules as that session left
  them (RulesMonitorService::OnExtensionLoaded, one load for both), so
  until this launch's first update ends the exceptions in force are that
  session's.
- At launch, the blocker that was ready in the last session is the one
  loaded again (security CB-02, addendum 4). After a list update that is
  the second blocker, and loading the first, which has no state, would
  start every such launch without lists. FinishTakeover writes the bundle
  version into the ready blocker's extension preferences
  ("rearc_ready_bundle") and Unload removes it, with the blocker's dynamic
  rules. At launch ApplyState loads the one slot whose marker is the bundle
  in use, and only that one, so Chromium brings its lists and dynamic rules
  up with its load. No marker, a marker of another version, a value that is
  not a string or both slots marked is a fresh load in slot 0. The host can
  speak for a profile before the bundle it named is copied: ApplyState then
  has a level and no bundle, loads nothing and leaves the markers alone. It
  unloads, and removes markers, only when blocking is off. (It used to
  unload in both cases, so such a launch lost its marker and loaded fresh:
  after a list update every next launch, where the copy of the bundle
  before is deleted first and the bundle comes later than the profile.)
- A fresh load all the same (a new built-in bundle, blocking switched on
  from another Mac, a profile that never had a blocker) leaves restored
  pages unblocked until its lists are indexed. While a profile that wants
  blocking (level above 0) has no ready blocker, FreshLoadThrottle
  (ChromeContentBrowserClient::CreateThrottlesForNavigation) holds its http
  and https navigations, main frames and subframes, private windows
  following their profile. One limit per load, 1.5 s from the first
  navigation held, shared by every navigation, redirect and reload. It lets
  them go when the blocker is ready, when a remembered blocker's lists are
  on (OnRememberedListsLoaded), at the limit, when blocking is switched off
  and when the profile goes; it never cancels one. After that the profile's
  navigations are not held again until a fresh load starts. No throttle is
  created for a profile with blocking off or one the host has said nothing
  about, and a list update, with the old blocker ready, holds nothing.

What a list may do (security CB-03). PrepareBundle reads every rules file
while it copies it and refuses the bundle when a rule's action is not
block, allow or allowAllRequests, with the JSON reader that indexes the
rules. The manifest the engine builds asks for declarativeNetRequest and
nothing else: no host permissions, no content scripts. That is what makes
Chromium ignore redirect and header rules from the blocker today;
engine/tests/check_content_blocker_manifest.py fails when a key or a
permission is added, and the check on the rules keeps a signed list to
block and allow also once the manifest gains access to pages (phase 2).

Site exceptions are two dynamic rules with fixed IDs at priority 1000000:
allowAllRequests for main_frame and sub_frame requests to the hosts, and
allow for requests the hosts' documents initiate. Own rules take IDs from
1000 and priorities 11 to 110, above the lists' 1 to 4; only block, allow
and allowAllRequests are accepted, so synced own rules can never redirect a
request or change headers.

Blocked counts are ActionTracker's per-tab action count, which a main frame
navigation resets (the sum of both blockers' while a list update has two; a
request is blocked by one of them). With the "action count as badge text" preference set for
the blocker, ActionTracker hands each change to
ExtensionsAPIClient::UpdateActionCount; ChromeExtensionsAPIClient passes the
blocker's to rearc::OnContentBlockerActionCount (it has no ExtensionAction
to badge), which maps the extension tab ID to the registry tab.
ReArcTabGetBlockedCount reads it, and ReArcEngineSetBlockedCountCallback
hears changes together at most every 250 ms.

Built in out/ReArc-codecs-arm64 (QA build 995, then again on 2026-11-18
with the remembered slot and FreshLoadThrottle, and once more with the
markers kept while the bundle is copied). The security review ran
the load, the levels, the exceptions, the own rules and the counts on build
995 (security/PRE-content-blocking.md, addendum 4). The takeover on a list
update and the hold need the self-test steps of the spec's section 8.

Touches: chrome/app/framework.exports, chrome/app/framework.order,
chrome/browser/BUILD.gn, chrome/browser/chrome_content_browser_client.cc,
chrome/browser/extensions/api/chrome_extensions_api_client.cc,
chrome/browser/extensions/component_extensions_allowlist/allowlist.cc,
chrome/browser/rearc_bridge/{rearc_abi_internal.h, rearc_content_blocker.cc
(new), rearc_content_blocker.h (new), rearc_engine_abi.h,
rearc_engine_abi.mm, rearc_engine_entry_mac.mm, rearc_extension_actions.cc,
rearc_profile_registry.cc}.

Bridge 0.76.0, ABI 1.66, feature content_blocking. No struct changes.

Last rebased: 2026-11-18 onto Chromium 156.0.8078.109 (overlay 2ba09186f1).

