+// content_blocking (ABI 1.66): built-in ad and tracker blocking. The host
+// verifies a blocklist bundle (signature, file hashes) and names its
+// directory. The engine copies the bundle's rules into the user data
+// directory, because declarativeNetRequest writes its index next to the rules
+// and the host's copy may be inside the signed app, and loads them as the
+// static rulesets of a component extension, "ReArc Blocker", in every profile
+// the host has switched blocking on for. Nothing here is the source of truth:
+// the host says which lists, which sites are excepted and which rules are the
+// person's own, at profile load and on every change. A profile the host has
+// said nothing about has no blocker.
+//
+// There are two such extensions, with two IDs (kContentBlockerExtensionIds),
+// and in ordinary times one of them is loaded. On a list update the new
+// bundle is loaded as the other one, and the old one goes on blocking until
+// the new one's lists are on: an extension cannot change its rulesets'
+// files without a reload, and a reload leaves every open page unblocked
+// while 18,000 rules are indexed (security CB-02). A blocker's lists are
+// switched on after its site exceptions and own rules are set, never before.
+//
+// At launch the blocker that was ready in the last session, for the bundle in
+// use, is loaded again (a marker in its extension preferences says which), so
+// that Chromium brings its lists and dynamic rules up with the extension's
+// load. While a profile that wants blocking has no ready blocker all the
+// same (a new bundle, blocking switched on elsewhere, a profile that never
+// had a blocker), its web navigations are held until the lists are on, for
+// at most kFreshLoadHold per load (FreshLoadThrottle; security CB-02).
